= Status quo bias =

== Overview ==
'''Status quo bias''' is a disproportionate preference for maintaining an existing state or previously selected option.

== Possible mechanisms ==
Observed status-quo preference can reflect loss aversion, switching costs, uncertainty, regret avoidance, implied endorsement, effort, or informational value. The behavioral effect is not a single mechanism.

== Normative caution ==
Remaining with the status quo can be rational when change has real costs. Bias claims require controlling those costs or specifying the normative benchmark.

== Default states and decision-system evaluation ==
A default option can affect the observed selection of an action, but a persistent choice is not automatically irrational. To diagnose a status quo bias, a model must specify the available alternatives, the current or default state, switching costs, uncertainty, information, and a defensible decision benchmark. Rational persistence can follow from genuine constraints.

This is relevant to [[Related to::Decision theory|decision-system design]] because '''state and default-policy representation''' change the problem a decision maker faces. A researcher can compare behavior when a choice is framed with different default states while holding important consequences constant. The result is a testable hypothesis about human choice under specified conditions.

In software and agent systems, defaults are explicit product or policy choices. The human status quo bias literature does not by itself show that autonomous agents possess the same preference; effects must be tested under the agent's actual selection rule and costs.

== References ==
